#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158141} :activity/id #uuid "b01ba0f8-312d-5711-866c-f427e34e9a49" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051016 :topic/id #uuid "7a5cb63d-dea0-503e-adc1-145b6400b020" :topic/math-academy-id 2835} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158486 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to compute the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\\,\\mathrm d A}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is a washer enclosed between two circles centered at $(0,0)$ with radii $1$ and $2,$ respectively, as shown below.\n\n![](images/47/4752982aaf806b9e3b9c4d28d7a92bd01c3ff6a68afcca4235061a01b2fbc86f.png)\n\nThe easiest way to solve this problem is to convert it to polar coordinates. Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe preimage of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its preimage $\\Delta$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0cc6c7f92704365c3ce15b91d85f703330b767a01f1f773b014cf61620563a3.png)\n\n             \n\nThe change of variables formula for polar coordinates states \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot [{2}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot 7 \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\\,\\mathrm d A}} = \\dfrac{14\\pi}{3}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "26614628-ce8b-5afe-ac90-b277c7c0f887" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4466 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0aaacbc4-1148-50d7-a5cf-50570ae0a925" :step/math-academy-id 27487 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158144}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158142 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157298 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157102 :question/id #uuid "189d117b-b510-59dd-8340-a84bd1c08472" :question/math-academy-id "e-7409"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433780}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "a6d6c9e7-5ab7-5550-b9f5-44b7f15426b5" :step/math-academy-id 27488 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158143}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158143 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157299 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157103 :question/id #uuid "859e1d71-21cb-5e30-9d60-a4b21358fe1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-7410"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436642}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"} :step/id #uuid "659f011d-8169-5c22-b20a-0f19271882cd" :step/math-academy-id 27489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158144 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157300 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157104 :question/id #uuid "ac36f233-1f32-5cba-a35e-aa8e48207a18" :question/math-academy-id "e-7411"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f13c3a8-ba48-5299-b335-3752e8b92ef4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424331}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "cd3a2583-f9bf-5dcc-9d80-5572b021e950" :step/math-academy-id 27493 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158147}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158147 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158487 :tutorial/content "Let's get some more practice at expressing regions in polar coordinates. \n\nConsider the region enclosed by the curve $x^2+(y-2)^2=4$ and the line $y=2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/df/dffcd94cc96c33d173704a63cb03fb0f0098bc67d6c66a855c16f59694bde8e1.png)\n\nThis region represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, we recall the usual transformations from polar to Cartesian coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now use the above formulas to write the functions that bound our region in polar coordinates:\n\n- For $x^2+(y-2)^2 = 4,$  we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ ({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = 4\\sin\\theta.$\n\n- For $y=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ \n\nFirst of all, note that if we draw an arrow through the region from the origin, it enters the region via the curve $r=2\\csc\\theta$ and exits via $r=4\\sin\\theta \\mathbin{:}$  \n\n![](images/b0/b09ab4e4e08dfb056964947dc866b965940b761d70c945faf001446c1df7869f.png)\n\nSecondly, our curves intersect at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(2,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/6a/6a132d5f8c58ce60c794595ee80c57c5b9b02e630004808cd53a7677efd022e7.png)\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 2\\csc\\theta \\leq r \\leq 4\\sin\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\Big\\}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "870d0884-b353-5292-b2e5-593725024502" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4472 :tutorial/title "Writing More Complex Regions in Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "098b5c2c-68e3-5938-bf5f-621a688c8ba7" :step/math-academy-id 27531 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158142}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Between Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157490}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436643} :activity/id #uuid "329c6544-9aa2-54c0-9a3b-55a4f1be88d7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051016 :topic/id #uuid "7a5cb63d-dea0-503e-adc1-145b6400b020" :topic/math-academy-id 2835} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b7bcdd2-a4aa-5f5a-8f98-2422d1091a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352783} {:answer/id #uuid "ac919fed-1071-5ed1-ac5f-813f92578d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353032} {:answer/id #uuid "afa4880d-8495-5e0c-a30f-f4a9c595deec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353064} {:answer/id #uuid "b0156a3c-6e2e-5c24-8878-51857602147d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353073} {:answer/id #uuid "d18eeca6-da7c-589c-a965-b05476689840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353381}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353064} :answer-field/id #uuid "87a5bb53-0890-5adf-a609-e4bea366a209" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9578f94f-5956-53e9-ae71-c25041b507cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-83999" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0d72be437a462f5eea781e83cc2a95e956e11ddc3295ad3b02a4a57867d9271.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(\\sqrt{3},-1).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/23/2386c1dcb1cf71231f2bb508dc1dd85dadef15b806eaf7f9a9ad0d3e4f3da1ca.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6} \\\\  & =[(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(0-\\pi )] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "33c2babf-7e9f-5c44-9eaf-3599485acba6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d8f91d3-c985-5ff9-a7d5-30ebbe848335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352276} {:answer/id #uuid "616be36f-20a7-5594-9222-5abdb16a236b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352438} {:answer/id #uuid "79385752-aec6-56af-a9f5-88f94e3cb797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352624} {:answer/id #uuid "9294ec35-9104-50c2-abc4-83106e810f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352836} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ba7cbd-8617-5aa0-9cf3-e77cd9c04b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352624} :answer-field/id #uuid "379a280a-d1e2-57b2-b66e-8dfa34c9f758" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c5f310f9-e63f-56fe-abce-21ad9b7c4099" :question/math-academy-id "q-85065" :question/problem "![](images/15/15e58073c87bd249473950680a49e8fc4cb4be94277a7001c0ab9f96ab3a46df.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $x=-3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(-2,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-3 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-3,-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(-3,\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/92/928c6dc79f44a2185ccf217c0cf9210630b6554a698f805ef2c77500f9ddc76b.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "51af2359-06ac-53f8-aa4f-f5e32f6dd195" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04830521-3355-5cb5-969c-1650b3f830fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351611} {:answer/id #uuid "100cff3b-1934-5849-b10d-7ff26075848e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351704} {:answer/id #uuid "148a4d56-41a6-57ea-804c-a991e5584c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351745} {:answer/id #uuid "82ffbdc0-51d3-5215-90bd-435914ccdf5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352712} {:answer/id #uuid "f86b2a50-5585-5203-b724-06d25c9c5e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352712} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b9760e9-c40f-5ec5-866a-aa3fc636ddf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a399d3dc-47f2-5985-b430-d143a3ff87d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-85182" :question/problem "![](images/51/51439321ae9d3760eb48d54351dbd519efe290c77d9197ca55aa590ce79e91a8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $x=3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =0,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/bf/bf2d20acbb93d3c61e9890a49d1c15a08c2b4e18b474da421bded7cf3dafc297.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "590ece9b-2d9c-50b1-94a0-0c369f325824" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013b7b16-b535-5412-8ea8-edd8fe941911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351575} {:answer/id #uuid "0a07701f-e9ed-5bcd-8b82-07920ae0db5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351654} {:answer/id #uuid "7747850c-b635-58ff-becc-cebb5b7aea70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352612} {:answer/id #uuid "9051911f-0beb-5472-ab3e-0a639b001b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352817} {:answer/id #uuid "c4342d60-22f3-5739-b274-49639ed612bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352817} :answer-field/id #uuid "39b5990a-8cb3-59df-a9d3-5b1bc0c05823" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e2773090-aaa0-5b0f-ab6e-ecfc1a50c98f" :question/math-academy-id "q-85206" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf7089294bc72330065590a9fe54acebee6d0ab982bffb4a162407c7232898ec.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/1b/1b83e2674bba4bd6c326083f8c94496b5288de23575dc63a92b9a660eba69f67.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )] \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c4072710-5fb0-565b-81f2-33d2fc972335" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436647}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436642 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f9894f-9b1a-5900-9728-e89ee154ca0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{254\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436636} {:answer/id #uuid "68aa1ac1-e8a7-508d-88c0-b565a4e94a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{508\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436637} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccd92ca-3dd3-51a1-b84b-9d383e7fe955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{255\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436638} {:answer/id #uuid "91f7c777-0592-5298-8f5a-8cd55fbdab32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{510\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436639} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8af1eb-e88d-5f7f-b4bb-c743c4774a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436637} :answer-field/id #uuid "77ba2466-990e-5f7d-96e7-58f6c3675c84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "50c86907-f8d2-5518-873d-192b1784708d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161119" :question/problem "![](images/a1/a16e4559960b923535cadecb1e623592c07fa11de5929e722cffb85679195a66.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/4}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $4,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/4}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})}^{3/4}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}({r}^{2})^{3/4}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}{r}^{3/2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}{r}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{2}{7}{r}^{7/2}]}_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(128-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{254}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{254}{7}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{254}{7}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{508\\pi }{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d00b50a9-8e9b-5ecf-92b4-773bdb165274"}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Between Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436635}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490358} :activity/id #uuid "a24b5247-c2da-5645-9e05-03878357cafa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051016 :topic/id #uuid "7a5cb63d-dea0-503e-adc1-145b6400b020" :topic/math-academy-id 2835} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490331 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14619b02-c3bb-5be6-85af-30af1494c733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424298} {:answer/id #uuid "441c7356-d182-50d5-a1bd-8b54901c3f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424303} {:answer/id #uuid "7f18edf8-e5d2-5da6-9302-024ff289f750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424311} {:answer/id #uuid "91bb664e-dbb4-5152-b157-eaf96ebfee48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{26\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424313} {:answer/id #uuid "f40e93e6-995f-501f-964e-89e8ceda688e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{52\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424321} :answer-field/id #uuid "64089c5c-af77-5ef9-b56b-0b6dff4fd9ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45cc9fce-954c-547e-835b-7dec4ec6d636" :question/math-academy-id "q-161103" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a56bcec93b5cc66ef8238b7fe7b213c5e184f1cf4b11baf4524525a63a284780.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(9-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{52\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "032a341e-0565-552a-b03a-ffb8b061f234"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63ff2307-bdea-53b3-88e5-97b96d5ad36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489749} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4cd776-9de6-538f-9d94-7a3efcb04fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489772} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4aa5b4-4bf8-52ba-bb0c-de13688b61cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489774} {:answer/id #uuid "c761b339-ab83-5bb8-80db-4cc0d6bebede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489946} {:answer/id #uuid "cce7b078-805e-5d09-a2f3-9ad87e8072c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489969} :answer-field/id #uuid "d25f125e-94ad-5225-b5e5-f24049a7a3e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d182d5c-4098-5732-b594-84e4e6e5bea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-83789" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b694a0907a5cb1c33d44c921b01b8e351059ce3e695485d79b72051f7ed764a.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and the line $y=1,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/68/68f70968b0dd256159dbfdcbfde4f2f63578237db1770e019c8fb19a5a638eaa.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6f29dc74-d09a-5b6e-a542-bfc991375287" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490382}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "939507be-1c81-5ae7-8ee3-1c8b4987b74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155962} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0de5db-9466-5fa3-9728-3739822adce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155966} {:answer/id #uuid "339ef220-3e41-588b-8376-adbd3ff3fd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489630} {:answer/id #uuid "68754c06-e278-58cb-aad9-4cc8f6c4502c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489765} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f50510-55ec-59ec-bc2f-bcefacaba339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489630} :answer-field/id #uuid "801f0ec1-f516-55ae-acb8-d095f0095e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "932bfdfe-cb1f-5acf-bcb0-ab10a83a0361" :question/math-academy-id "q-83986" :question/problem "![](images/df/dfaa18cb3295d3bb1641f440a3c1642758fe793c3605cf7d010121a68bbff47d.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion between the disks of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/46/4660d61851814c87afc7c140dd87e1354785f55cafe4fbc911fd369f768914e3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{4-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[-\\frac{1}{3}(4-{r}^{2})^{3/2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{3}(0-3\\sqrt{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9fcfe624-e47d-5c6a-bcb3-629540a17648" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490385}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490382 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18a268e5-f9a7-5175-82f6-429e900d7180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394560} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8c7c83-2673-55cc-b80f-82dd032a52b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394561} {:answer/id #uuid "781c89a4-4fc2-5fdd-81c9-1fe0439559bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394562} {:answer/id #uuid "c04067e2-1051-59f7-ba78-7d685ba5c6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394563} {:answer/id #uuid "7e63b9da-492f-5a21-ae92-0344b2806a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394564}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394560} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e89d210-e453-5f03-84ad-9efe39e4cea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "95f91c79-4c92-5753-8c02-6dfc6c0682b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-83980" :question/problem "![](images/92/928fb72015b78436f95895799c2846d1a0b34242b7216d31a6ed6bdda46728a1.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ and the line $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/37/375e270ce444d9f62b50f9a25abfae781113fdad895833d693061ffffe93f5d3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d044a436-919a-5bca-b4cc-859a4b8341a4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490371}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490385 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424328 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "055b0e6b-d664-5538-b782-3dfbe82f8ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424297} {:answer/id #uuid "320a2c42-e7e8-5a89-b8ed-772fad2d8845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424302} {:answer/id #uuid "4e20bf88-0220-51b3-8e06-d32a2b7d8220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424305} {:answer/id #uuid "776ef258-4be4-56d1-84fa-73c7f0f2fd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424309} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6a190c-ce1c-504a-9a43-1fa603eb0266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424317}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424297} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc66dd31-0a57-5231-b848-a0871bc42f3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "78459587-308f-5867-9175-f728a41ccebc" :question/math-academy-id "q-85181" :question/problem "![](images/3d/3d5542933934102149c58153c3108d55d9c615e991db8aca8d8fc1e60837917f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ and the line $y=3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-\\sqrt{3},3).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/49/4903ca2ba8cde3f7874e10998a0741035ec7b2dea69a7804eaca64cb552fc572.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ea22112c-a2f7-5185-ac77-d7f0838d3983" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490331}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Between Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489507}]}
